# What `railway up` must NOT upload.
#
# The Dockerfile does `COPY . .`, so the upload IS the build context. A git-
# triggered deploy only ever sees tracked files, but `railway up` sends the
# working directory — including untracked scratch dirs that have no business in
# a production image and make the upload slow enough to look hung.
#
# Everything listed here is provably unnecessary to
# `dotnet publish Ruumly.Backend/Ruumly.Backend.csproj`, which is the only thing
# the Dockerfile builds.

# The frontend is a separate repo with its own Vercel deploy. It is also by far
# the largest thing in this directory (node_modules + dist).
estonia-space-hub/

# Cloudflare Worker — deployed with wrangler, not with the API.
workers/

# Scratch and staging directories that live in the working tree untracked.
.codex-video-staging/
_screens_backup/
.tmp-kling-login.err
.tmp-kling-login.out

# Local-only compose file; Railway provides its own Postgres.
docker-compose.yml

# Docs and audit reports — read by people, not by the runtime.
docs/
*.md

# Build output. Uploading these risks shipping a stale binary into the image,
# which is the exact failure mode `dotnet clean` exists to undo locally.
**/bin/
**/obj/

# Agent/session scratch.
.claude/

.git/
.vs/
.vscode/
